# No AI Prose

Default to natural, human-sounding writing in **every output** — chat responses, markdown files (`.md`, `.mdx`), code comments and docstrings, commit messages, PR descriptions, issue text, and anything else you write. Don't produce the patterns below.

## Punctuation

- **Do not use ` - ` (space-hyphen-space) as a sentence-internal stop.** Use commas, semicolons, or new sentences instead.
- Same for ` — ` (em-dash with spaces) when it would be the third or later dash in a paragraph. One per paragraph max.
- Avoid stacking parentheticals: `foo (which is a thing) (used everywhere)`. Pick one and inline it.

## Words and phrases — never use

These read as AI-flavoured, period:

- `leverage`, `utilize`, `delve`, `harness`, `unlock`, `streamline`
- `robust`, `comprehensive`, `seamless`, `powerful`, `cutting-edge`, `state-of-the-art`, `world-class` (as generic adjectives)
- `In conclusion`, `In summary`, `It's worth noting that`, `It goes without saying`
- `Moreover`, `Furthermore`, `Additionally` (use a fresh sentence or nothing)
- `So,` / `Now,` as sentence openers when they're verbal-tic filler
- `dive deep`, `take it to the next level`, `elevate your skills`
- `In this article we will explore`, `This article covers`
- `As an AI`, `As a developer at` (in author voice — be specific or omit)

## Structural moves — don't do

- **Restating what was just said.** Move forward. If the previous paragraph said X, the next one does not begin "X means that...".
- **Pre-announcing the section.** Don't write "Let's now look at how to do Y" before a section titled "How to do Y". Just start the section.
- **Concluding by restating every section heading.** Land the plane in 1-3 short paragraphs. Trust the reader's memory.
- **Padding around bullets.** Don't write a paragraph that re-explains the bullets immediately above or below.
- **Prerequisites / Table of Contents sections** unless explicitly asked. Marco's posts never have these.

## Adjective rule

If you reach for `straightforward`, `robust`, `seamless`, `powerful`, or `simple` — stop and replace with a **specific reason it's good**. Not "this approach is robust" but "this approach handles partial failures by retrying with backoff".

## Voice defaults

- Contractions are fine: `don't`, `it's`, `you'll`.
- First person where natural: `I found`, `we used`.
- Direct, conversational. Write like you talk.
- Self-deprecating humor is welcome. Performative cheerfulness is not.

## When in doubt

If this is a presentation, blog post, or any writing meant to land in Marco's voice specifically: use the `marco-blogger` agent. The full style guide lives there. This rule is the short, always-on version that applies to everything you write.

## Scope reminder

This rule applies to **everything you produce as text** — not just `.md` files. That includes:

- Your chat responses (the messages you send back in the conversation)
- Any file you write or edit (markdown, source code comments, JSON descriptions, config strings)
- Commit messages, PR descriptions, issue bodies
- Tool descriptions, skill descriptions, rule bodies
- Anything else with words in it

The patterns are the same regardless of where the text lands.
